Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Secondary amenorrhoea, Nausea and vomiting, Positive serum/urine hCG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-040
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of continuous nausea, vomiting, and extreme fatigue following 8 weeks of amenorrhoea.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, unremitting nausea and vomiting causing an inability to tolerate fluids for 12 hours, accompanied by acute anxiety regarding the cause of these symptoms following a missed period. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively diagnose early pregnancy, rule out acute gastroenteritis or appendicitis, and administer parenteral antiemetics to prevent dehydration before the next available in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have not had a period in two months, and today I have been vomiting constantly, my breasts are incredibly sore, and I cannot keep water down.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the cessation of previously regular menses (secondary amenorrhoea) for the past 8 weeks. She describes a progressive sensation of morning sickness, nausea, and vomiting that has acutely escalated today, rendering her unable to tolerate oral fluids for the past 12 hours. She complains of tender, swollen breasts with darkening of the areolae and extreme generalized fatigue. She notes increased urinary frequency unassociated with dysuria. She denies abdominal pain, fever, or diarrhea.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe nausea and lethargy.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; risk of progressive dehydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, fatigued, and is holding an emesis basin. Mildly dehydrated (dry lips).
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft, completely non-tender, with no localized guarding or rebound. Bowel sounds are normal. Pelvic examination (external inspection) shows normal anatomy. A point-of-care highly sensitive urine beta-hCG test is immediately and strongly positive. Urinalysis shows specific gravity of 1.025 (mild dehydration) but is negative for leukocytes, nitrites, or significant ketones.
Environmental Observations: Clean apartment; water bottle by the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Normal Early Pregnancy (First Trimester) complicated by acute hyperemesis - Status: Acute nausea flare
Differentials:

Acute Gastroenteritis - Definitively ruled out by the positive pregnancy test, the chronic progressive nature of the nausea (over weeks), and the complete absence of fever or diarrhea.

Ectopic Pregnancy - While the patient is pregnant, the complete absence of unilateral abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, or severe hemodynamic collapse makes an uncomplicated intrauterine pregnancy much more likely, though a dating scan is required.

Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out by the negative urinalysis; the urinary frequency is a physiological sign of early pregnancy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic diagnostic tetrad of early pregnancy: secondary amenorrhoea, severe nausea/vomiting, and a strongly positive urine hCG test. The objective physical examination safely ruled out an acute surgical abdomen. While this is a normal physiological process, the acute severity of the vomiting, leading to an inability to tolerate oral fluids and mild dehydration, required urgent after-hours intervention. Administering an immediate parenteral antiemetic was necessary to break the nausea cycle, allow for oral rehydration, and prevent an otherwise inevitable emergency department transfer for intravenous fluids prior to the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular metoclopramide 10mg to rapidly suppress the acute vomiting. Provided a routine referral for a formal blood beta-hCG quantitative test and a dating transvaginal ultrasound. Prescribed oral doxylamine/pyridoxine combination to commence tonight for long-term nausea management.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain hydration without triggering your gag reflex, take only one small sip of water every 15 minutes; experiencing the ability to keep this tiny amount down will reinforce the habit of micro-dosing fluids. To prevent the nausea from spiking in the morning, keep plain dry crackers on your nightstand and eat two before you even sit up in bed; noticing the reduction in morning sickness will validate this dietary adjustment.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in one week to discuss ultrasound results, arrange formal antenatal care, and monitor weight/hydration.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if you become completely unable to hold down fluids for another 12 hours despite the medication, if you develop severe, sharp lower abdominal pain, or if you begin bleeding heavily from the vagina.

